- helm
- net
- mesa_foot

' This module is the packet pump of the foot at /io/mesa. It has the actor
' that owns the socket, and the loop that runs the rules of the foot. The pump
' does these things:
' - It takes the packets that the socket holds and gives them to the rules.
' - It sends the packets that the rules make (hints). The engine sends its
'   own packets through the socket.
' - It tells the rules the time when the engine needs it, and at least each
'   second.
' - It asks the world to make each change that the rules ask for, one after
'   the other (mesa_wire/wish).
' - It tells Mesa the key that each card names for a peer. Mesa decides if it
'   accepts that key, and Mesa holds what it accepted.
'
' No code here makes a decision about a page. The rules are in mesa_foot and
' net. No code here holds a key: Mesa holds the key of the world and the key
' that it accepted for each peer.
'
' The record of the foot, /<self>/io/mesa, tells where the foot listens and
' where two of its files are:
' - `port`
' - `ring` (the file of the key of the world, 32 bytes that only Mesa reads)
' - `mark` (its rift, the highest epoch that it announced, and the lowest
'   revision of its key manifest that it accepts)
' - `life` (the number of the key of the world; a higher number makes the
'   foot change to a new key)
'
' These files are intentionally outside the world, in io/mesa/:
' - the mark
' - the key file, and one more file for each later key (`ring.2`, ...)
' - `ring.manifest`: which key Mesa signs with for the world
' - `ring.authority`: what Mesa accepted for each peer, and each block
'
' The ring names the place of all that Mesa keeps for the world.
'
' A restore of the world does not take them back. Thus a restore is seen (the
' mark), an old key of the world is not used again (the manifest), and a key
' of a peer that Mesa replaced or blocked is not accepted again (the
' authority).
'
' The card of each peer, /<self>/io/mesa/<peer>, tells the `key` that this
' world wants Mesa to accept for that peer, with its `life` and its `rift`
' (when absent: 1 and 0), and the `host` and the `port` where the peer is
' reached. The card is a request. If Mesa does not accept it, the foot writes
' the reason beside the card (`stand`), and the peer is not a peer of the
' foot.
'
' Packets go between worlds as UDP datagrams on one socket. The socket gives
' the peer of a packet from the address that the packet came from, and it
' discards a packet from an address that no card names. The foot does not
' believe an address: Mesa examines each page with the key that it accepted.
'
' The foot stops when its record is removed or holds `stop`. It stops the
' actor that waits on the socket, closes the socket and the engine, and then
' stops.
+ mesa_wire
  + tick
    ' The actor that waits on a socket tells the foot the time. `serial` is
    ' the number of the socket that the actor waits on. `ready` is 1 when the
    ' socket holds a packet, 0 when the time passed, and 2 when the socket is
    ' closed.
    : now=nat serial=nat ready=nat
  + again
    ' The foot asks itself for a new engine (mesa_foot/effect/renew).
    : era=nat
  + task
    & feet/task/ack
      feet/task/fact
      feet/task/binary
      mesa_wire/tick
      mesa_wire/wish
      mesa_wire/again
  + wish
    ' This is a change that a part of the foot asks the world to make
    ' (mesa_foot/change). The foot sends it to its own mailbox. Thus each
    ' part of the foot can ask, and the loop of the foot asks for one change
    ' at a time.
    '
    ' A write of this world is one poke. A landing is planned on the world as
    ' it is when its turn comes (mesa_wire/ask, mesa_wire/plan), and is then
    ' one land command. The foot asks for no other change until the answer
    ' comes. Thus each answer is the answer to the first wish that waits.
    : request=nat change=mesa_foot/change
  + whole
    ' This is the read that gives the world as it is now. A committed world
    ' is in the store. Thus the transfer of the world copies no data.
    \ state=namespace
    ^ [nat any nat]
    [200 state 0]
  + world
    ' This function gives the answer of mesa_wire/whole its type.
    \ body=any
    ^ namespace
    body
  + look
    ' This function asks the supervisor for the world as it is now.
    \ parent=nat
    ^ nat
    | Send parent (feet/gift/snapshot_bat 0 mesa_wire/whole)
  + ask
    ' This function starts the first wish that waits, if there is one. It
    ' sends a write as it is. For a landing, it asks for the world as it is
    ' now, with the request number 1: the plan of the landing is made on
    ' that world (mesa_wire/plan).
    \ parent=nat queue=row[mesa_wire/wish]
    ^ nat
    ? !(queue.len) 0
    ? queue.at(0).change
     > (./write target change) (Send parent (feet/gift/poke target change))
     > (./land root card epic) (Send parent (feet/gift/snapshot_bat 1 mesa_wire/whole))
  + plan
    ' This function runs when the world comes for a landing that waits first.
    ' It plans the landing on that world (net/land) and sends the plan as one
    ' land command. When the rules do not accept the epic, there is no
    ' command: the function gives the reason, as the answer to the wish.
    \ parent=nat queue=row[mesa_wire/wish] state=namespace
    ^ maybe[iota]
    = none=maybe[iota] .none
    ? !(queue.len) none
    ?> (./land root card epic) queue.at(0).change none
    ? (net/land root card epic state)
     > (./err why) (./some why)
     > (./ok made)
      ; (Send parent (feet/gift/land root.at(0) made.base made.diffs made.writes))
      none
  + note
    ' This function gives the wish that pokes a record on the node of the
    ' foot.
    \ home=path word=myth
    ^ mesa_wire/wish
    | mesa_wire/wish 0 (mesa_foot/change/write home (writ/poke word))
  + waiter
    ' The waiter waits on the socket of the foot. It tells the foot when the
    ' socket holds a packet, or when the time that the foot gave it passed.
    ' Then it waits for the answer of the foot: the number of milliseconds to
    ' wait next, or 0 to stop. A socket is a number, and thus this actor can
    ' wait on the socket that the foot owns.
    \ fd=nat serial=nat self=nat
    ^ nat
    = ;[hello caps] (Recv[nat] 0)
      foot caps.at(0)
    % wait=nat 1
    = ;ready (MesaWait fd wait)
    ; (? (eq 2 ready) (Sleep 1) 0)
    ; (SendCaps foot (mesa_wire/tick (div (Now 0) 1000000) serial ready) [0])
    = ;[go more] (Recv[nat] 0)
    ; (helm/supervisor/close_caps more)
    ? !(go) 0
    | _ go
  + watch
    ' This function starts the waiter of a socket.
    \ fd=nat serial=nat
    ^ nat
    = ;helper (Spawn (mesa_wire/waiter fd serial))
    ; (SendCaps helper 0 [0])
    0
  + route
    ' This is a peer as its card gives it: the key that this world wants Mesa
    ' to accept for the peer, with its life and its rift, and the address
    ' where the peer is reached.
    : world=iota id=nat key=nat life=nat rift=nat host=str port=nat
  + routes
    ' This function gives the peers that a binding names: each child that
    ' holds a key and an address.
    \ binding=axal[saga]
    ^ row[mesa_wire/route]
    = none=row[mesa_wire/route] []
    | binding.kid_tap.fold none
      \ out=row[mesa_wire/route] [key tree]
      ^ row[mesa_wire/route]
      ?> (./some version) tree.fil out
      = card (unpin version.myth)
        held card.nat_at(['key] 0)
        port card.nat_at(['port] 0)
      ? (eq 0 held) out
      ? (eq 0 port) out
      | out.snoc((mesa_wire/route key (quip_val key) held card.nat_at(['life] 1) card.nat_at(['rift] 0) card.txt_at(['host]) port))
  + own
    \ binding=axal[saga]
    ^ myth
    ?> (./some version) binding.fil myth/new
    | unpin version.myth
  + link
    ' This is what the foot holds of a peer: the card that Mesa accepted (a
    ' key of 0: Mesa accepted none, or the card is gone), the numeric address
    ' of the peer (0: none), and the generation of its route. The generation
    ' is also the number that the engine knows the route by. It only
    ' increases, and thus the foot keeps the link of a peer whose card is
    ' gone.
    : world=iota id=nat key=nat life=nat rift=nat host=str port=nat address=nat gen=nat
  + peers
    ' This function gives the peers of the foot: the links that Mesa accepted
    ' a key for.
    \ links=row[mesa_wire/link]
    ^ row[mesa_foot/peer]
    = none=row[mesa_foot/peer] []
    | links.fold none
      \ out=row[mesa_foot/peer] item=mesa_wire/link
      ^ row[mesa_foot/peer]
      ? (eq 0 item.key) out
      | out.snoc((mesa_foot/peer item.world item.id))
  + why
    ' This function gives the reason that the kernel gave when it did not
    ' accept a poke. It gives none when the kernel accepted the poke.
    \ err=maybe[pail]
    ^ maybe[iota]
    = none=maybe[iota] .none
      unknown=iota 'net_refused
    ?> (./some refusal) err none
    ?> (./some said) (pail/as[helm/supervisor/rejection] refusal) (./some unknown)
    | ./some said.reason
  + act
    ' This function does what the rules ask. It sends each packet that the
    ' foot made to the address of its peer. It puts each change, and each
    ' request for a new engine, in the mailbox of the foot.
    \ endpoint=nat links=row[mesa_wire/link] does=row[mesa_foot/effect]
    ^ nat
    | does.fold 0
      \ ;done=nat effect=mesa_foot/effect
      ^ nat
      ? effect
       > (./send to packet)
        = found links.keep((\ item=mesa_wire/link (and (eq item.id to) (gt item.address 0))))
        ? !(found.len) done
        ; (MesaSend endpoint to found.at(0).gen packet)
        done.inc
       > (./admit request change)
        ; (Send 0 (mesa_wire/wish request change))
        done.inc
       > (./renew era)
        ; (Send 0 (mesa_wire/again era))
        done.inc
  + mark
    ' This is the rift of a world, the highest epoch that the world announced,
    ' and the lowest revision of its key manifest that the world accepts.
    : rift=nat top=nat floor=nat
  + marked
    ' This function gives the mark of a world. It gives zeros when there is
    ' no file. It gives none when the file does not hold a mark: zeros would
    ' then let the world use again a version that it published before.
    \ file=str
    ^ maybe[mesa_wire/mark]
    = ;bytes (mesa_wire/took file)
    ? (eq 0 bytes) (./some (mesa_wire/mark 0 0 0))
    ? !(eq (wid bytes) 25) .none
    | ./some (mesa_wire/mark (loadvar 0 8 bytes) (loadvar 8 8 bytes) (loadvar 16 8 bytes))
  + remark
    ' This function writes a mark. It gives 1 when the mark is whole on the
    ' disk (the runtime writes a new file and then gives it the name), and 0
    ' when it is not.
    \ file=str rift=nat top=nat floor=nat
    ^ nat
    = w64 (bex 64)
      body (add (add (mod rift w64) (lsh (mod top w64) 64)) (lsh (mod floor w64) 128))
    | MesaPersist (mesa_wire/spelled file) (add body (bex 192))
  + halt
    ' This function tells why the foot does not run, and stops the foot.
    \ why=str file=str
    ^ nat
    ; (Trace [why file] 0)
    0
  + named
    ' This function gives the path of a file as the foot's record gives it.
    ' When the record gives no path, the function gives the path where the
    ' file is kept.
    \ said=str kept=str
    ^ str
    ? (eq 0 said.len) kept
    said
  + took
    ' This function gives the bytes that a file holds, or no bytes.
    \ file=str
    ^ nat
    = ;held (Try ReadFile file)
    ? held
     > (./err _) 0
     > (./ok bytes) bytes
  + address
    ' This function gives the address where a world is reached. The one that
    ' started the world left the address for the foot in a file. The file has
    ' the name of the host and a port, as the one line "host port". The port
    ' is 0 when the file holds no such line. A world gets its address at each
    ' start, as it gets its founding at its first start. Thus, when a world is
    ' started in a different place, the world itself tells where it is.
    \ handed=nat
    ^ [host=str port=nat]
    = nothing=str ""
      none=[host=str port=nat] [nothing 0]
    ? (lt (wid handed) 2) none
    = text (str/from_atom (mod handed (bex (mul 8 (dec (wid handed))))))
      parts (str/split_byte 32 text)
    ? !(eq 2 parts.len) none
    ?> (./some port) (nat/parse parts.at(1)) none
    = got=[host=str port=nat] [parts.at(0) port]
    got
  + spelled
    ' This function gives a text as bytes, for the Mesa bridge.
    \ text=str
    ^ nat
    | mesa_foot/tag (str/to_atom text)
  + beside
    ' This function gives the file that Mesa keeps beside the key file of a
    ' world, as bytes for the bridge: the manifest of the keys, or the
    ' checkpoint of the authority.
    \ ring=str what=str
    ^ nat
    | mesa_wire/spelled (str/cat_all [ring "." what])
  + ring_of
    ' This function gives the file of the key of a world at a life. The first
    ' key is in the ring file. Each later key is in a file of its own.
    \ ring=str life=nat
    ^ str
    ? (le life 1) ring
    | str/cat_all [ring "." (nat/show life)]
  + revision
    ' This function gives the revision of the key manifest that an activation
    ' made, or 0 when the keys were not activated.
    \ acts=row[row[nat]]
    ^ nat
    | acts.fold 0
      \ out=nat act=row[nat]
      ^ nat
      ? (eq 6 act.at(0)) act.at(1)
      out
  + admit
    ' This function asks Mesa to accept what a card names for a peer. It gives
    ' 0 when Mesa holds that key, and the reason when Mesa does not accept it.
    ' Mesa does not accept a lower life or rift, or a different key at the
    ' same life. A peer whose card was removed is blocked, and the foot lifts
    ' the block only for a card with a higher life: an old card does not have
    ' one.
    \ authority=nat file=nat want=mesa_wire/route
    ^ nat
    = now (div (Now 0) 1000000)
      ;stood (MesaStanding authority want.id)
      blocked (eq 1 stood.at(5))
    ? (and blocked (le want.life stood.at(2))) (mesa_foot/tag "Blocked")
    = ;lifted (? blocked (MesaBlock authority file now want.id 0) 0)
    | MesaVouch authority file now want.id mesa_foot/domain want.life want.rift want.key
  + denied
    ' This function gives the write that records a card that Mesa did not
    ' accept: the key that the card names, and the reason. The record is in
    ' the place that keeps where the root of the peer stands, beside the
    ' card. The function gives no write when the record is there already.
    \ self=iota want=mesa_wire/route why=nat state=namespace
    ^ row[mesa_foot/effect]
    = card=path [self 'io 'mesa want.world 'stand]
      none=row[mesa_foot/effect] []
    ? (eq want.key (net/held ['barred] card state)) none
    = text (str/from_atom (mod why (bex (mul 8 (dec (wid why))))))
      said=myth (myth/sing ['barred] (pails/n want.key)).put(['cause] (pails/t text))
      change (? (net/live card state) (writ/poke said) (writ/make said))
    | none.snoc((mesa_foot/effect/admit 0 (mesa_foot/change/write card change)))
  + locked
    ' This function gives what this world shares with a peer. It gives none
    ' when Mesa does not serve that peer.
    \ keys=nat authority=nat id=nat
    ^ row[mesa_foot/lock]
    = none=row[mesa_foot/lock] []
      ;stood (MesaStanding authority id)
    ? !(eq 1 stood.at(4)) none
    = ;seal (MesaLock keys authority id (mesa_foot/tag "seal"))
      ;nonce (MesaLock keys authority id (mesa_foot/tag "nonce"))
      ;name (MesaLock keys authority id (mesa_foot/tag "name"))
    | none.snoc((mesa_foot/lock id seal nonce name))
  + fed
    ' This is what the foot holds after it told Mesa the cards: the links,
    ' what the world shares with each peer, what the engine told (reads that
    ' stopped), and the writes that record what Mesa did not accept.
    : links=row[mesa_wire/link] locks=row[mesa_foot/lock] acts=row[row[nat]] notes=row[mesa_foot/effect]
  + feed
    ' This function tells Mesa what the cards say, and gives the engine and
    ' the socket what Mesa then holds. For a card that is new or changed, it
    ' asks Mesa to accept the key (mesa_wire/admit) and gives the engine the
    ' answer. It sets the route of a peer whose address is new. For a card
    ' that is gone, it blocks the peer. It does no work for a card that did
    ' not change.
    \ wanted=row[mesa_wire/route] links=row[mesa_wire/link] keys=nat authority=nat file=nat state=namespace foot=mesa_foot/foot
    ^ mesa_wire/fed
    = no_links=row[mesa_wire/link] []
      no_locks=row[mesa_foot/lock] []
      no_acts=row[row[nat]] []
      no_notes=row[mesa_foot/effect] []
      nowhere=str ""
      ;kept
        | wanted.fold (mesa_wire/fed no_links no_locks no_acts no_notes)
          \ ;out=mesa_wire/fed want=mesa_wire/route
          ^ mesa_wire/fed
          = had links.keep((\ item=mesa_wire/link (eq item.id want.id)))
            old (? (gt had.len 0) had.at(0) (mesa_wire/link want.world want.id 0 0 0 nowhere 0 0 0))
            same (and (eq old.key want.key) (and (eq old.life want.life) (eq old.rift want.rift)))
            ;why (? same 0 (mesa_wire/admit authority file want))
          ? !(eq 0 why) (out.set_links(out.links.snoc(old.set_key(0))).set_notes(out.notes.weld((mesa_wire/denied state.self want why state))))
          = one=row[nat] [want.id]
            ;synced (? same no_acts (MesaSync authority foot.engine foot.endpoint one))
            placed (and (equal[str] old.host want.host) (eq old.port want.port))
            ;address (? placed old.address (MesaResolve (mesa_wire/spelled want.host) want.port))
            ;routed (? (placed || (eq 0 address)) 0 (MesaRoute foot.engine foot.endpoint want.id old.gen old.gen.inc address))
            next (mesa_wire/link want.world want.id want.key want.life want.rift (? routed want.host old.host) (? routed want.port old.port) (? routed address old.address) (? routed old.gen.inc old.gen))
            before foot.locks.keep((\ item=mesa_foot/lock (eq item.id want.id)))
            ;shared (? (and same (gt before.len 0)) before (mesa_wire/locked keys authority want.id))
          | out.set_links(out.links.snoc(next)).set_locks(out.locks.weld(shared)).set_acts(out.acts.weld(synced))
      now (div (Now 0) 1000000)
    | links.fold kept
      \ ;out=mesa_wire/fed item=mesa_wire/link
      ^ mesa_wire/fed
      ? (wanted.any((\ want=mesa_wire/route (eq want.id item.id)))) out
      ? (eq 0 item.key) (out.set_links(out.links.snoc(item)))
      = one=row[nat] [item.id]
        ;barred (MesaBlock authority file now item.id 1)
        ;synced (MesaSync authority foot.engine foot.endpoint one)
      | out.set_links(out.links.snoc(item.set_key(0))).set_acts(out.acts.weld(synced))
  + socket
    ' This is a socket and the number that a waiter waits on it by.
    : endpoint=nat fd=nat
  + bind
    ' This function makes the socket of a foot on a port (0: a port that the
    ' operating system selects). It gives the socket the routes of the links,
    ' at the generations that the engine knows them by.
    \ port=nat links=row[mesa_wire/link]
    ^ mesa_wire/socket
    = limits mesa_foot/limits
      ;endpoint (MesaEndpoint (mesa_wire/spelled (str/cat_all ["0.0.0.0:" (nat/show port)])) limits.at(0))
      ;fd (MesaSocket endpoint)
      ;tracked
        | links.fold 0
          \ ;done=nat item=mesa_wire/link
          ^ nat
          ? (eq 0 item.address) done
          ; (MesaTrack endpoint item.id 0 item.gen item.address)
          done.inc
    | mesa_wire/socket endpoint fd
  + drive
    ' This is an engine, what the engine told while it started, and the
    ' revision of the key manifest (0: Mesa did not activate the key, and the
    ' engine is closed).
    : engine=nat acts=row[row[nat]] floor=nat
  + engine
    ' This function makes the engine of a foot. The engine holds no key at
    ' first and is closed. Mesa then activates the key of the world for it:
    ' the manifest is durable before the engine signs. The engine gets what
    ' Mesa holds of each peer, and the routes that the socket has.
    \ self=iota life=nat links=row[mesa_wire/link] endpoint=nat keys=nat authority=nat manifest=nat
    ^ mesa_wire/drive
    = none=row[nat] []
      ;engine (MesaEngine (mesa_foot/id self) mesa_foot/domain mesa_foot/limits mesa_foot/bounds)
      ;acts (MesaActivate keys authority engine endpoint manifest life)
      ids
        | links.fold none
          \ out=row[nat] item=mesa_wire/link
          ^ row[nat]
          | out.snoc(item.id)
      ;synced (MesaSync authority engine endpoint ids)
      ;attached
        | links.fold 0
          \ ;done=nat item=mesa_wire/link
          ^ nat
          ? (eq 0 item.address) done
          ; (MesaAttach engine endpoint item.id item.gen)
          done.inc
    | mesa_wire/drive engine acts.weld(synced) (mesa_wire/revision acts)
  + turned
    ' This is the answer to a change of the key of the world: if Mesa changed
    ' it, the new public key, what the engine told, and the revision of the
    ' key manifest.
    : ok=nat public=nat acts=row[row[nat]] floor=nat
  + rotate
    ' This function changes the key of the world to the key of a higher life.
    ' Mesa makes the new key in a file of its own, accepts it for this world,
    ' and signs with it from then on. Then Mesa retires the old key, and the
    ' file of the old key is removed. Mesa closes the engine for each change
    ' of the manifest, and thus the function activates the new key a second
    ' time after the retirement.
    \ self=iota ring=str old=nat new=nat endpoint=nat engine=nat keys=nat authority=nat
    ^ mesa_wire/turned
    = me (mesa_foot/id self)
      none=row[row[nat]] []
      failed (mesa_wire/turned 0 0 none 0)
      manifest (mesa_wire/beside ring "manifest")
      ;got (MesaKeyImport keys (mesa_wire/spelled (mesa_wire/ring_of ring new)) new new 0 1)
    ? !(eq 2 got.len) failed
    = ;why (MesaVouch authority (mesa_wire/beside ring "authority") (div (Now 0) 1000000) me mesa_foot/domain new 0 got.at(0))
    ? !(eq 0 why) failed
    = ;first (MesaActivate keys authority engine endpoint manifest new)
    ? (eq 0 (mesa_wire/revision first)) (mesa_wire/turned 0 0 first 0)
    = ;retired (MesaRetire keys authority manifest old (mesa_wire/spelled (mesa_wire/ring_of ring old)))
      ;second (MesaActivate keys authority engine endpoint manifest new)
      floor (mesa_wire/revision second)
    ? (eq 0 floor) (mesa_wire/turned 0 0 first.weld(second) 0)
    | mesa_wire/turned 1 got.at(0) first.weld(second) floor
  + up
    ' This is a foot that started: the foot, its links, the handles on the
    ' keys and the authority that Mesa keeps for the world, the number of its
    ' socket, the revision of its key manifest, the life and the public key of
    ' the key that Mesa signs with, and what the rules ask for at the start.
    ' `why` is empty for a foot that started. If not, it is the reason that
    ' the foot did not start.
    : why=str foot=mesa_foot/foot links=row[mesa_wire/link] keys=nat authority=nat fd=nat floor=nat life=nat public=nat does=row[mesa_foot/effect]
  + raise
    ' This function starts a foot. It opens what Mesa keeps for the world
    ' beside the ring file: the authority and the keys. It makes the key of
    ' the world the first time (if the record states no key), asks Mesa to
    ' accept that key for the world, and makes the socket and the engine.
    ' Then it tells Mesa the cards.
    '
    ' The life of the key is the higher of two numbers: the life that the
    ' caller wants, and the life that Mesa holds for the world. Thus a world
    ' that is restored to an old record does not go back to an old key.
    \ ring=str port=nat rift=nat floor=nat wanted=nat stated=bool routes=row[mesa_wire/route] began=nat world=namespace
    ^ mesa_wire/up
    = me (mesa_foot/id world.self)
      no_links=row[mesa_wire/link] []
      no_does=row[mesa_foot/effect] []
      started=str ""
      idle (mesa_foot/start world.self rift 0 0)
      held (mesa_wire/beside ring "authority")
      kept_at (mesa_wire/beside ring "manifest")
      ;fresh (Entropy 16)
      incarnation (add 1 (mod fresh (bex 120)))
      ;checkpoint (mesa_wire/took (str/cat_all [ring ".authority"]))
      ;authority (MesaAuthority incarnation checkpoint held mesa_foot/domain began)
      ;manifest (mesa_wire/took (str/cat_all [ring ".manifest"]))
      ;keys (MesaKeys me mesa_foot/domain incarnation.inc 1 manifest floor)
      ;stood (MesaStanding authority me)
      life (nmax wanted stood.at(2))
      ;got (MesaKeyImport keys (mesa_wire/spelled (mesa_wire/ring_of ring life)) life life 0 (? stated 0 1))
    ? !(eq 2 got.len) (mesa_wire/up "the record states a key, and the file of that key is not there" idle no_links keys authority 0 0 life 0 no_does)
    = ;why (MesaVouch authority held began me mesa_foot/domain life 0 got.at(0))
    ? !(eq 0 why) (mesa_wire/up "Mesa does not accept the key of this world" idle no_links keys authority 0 0 life 0 no_does)
    = ;sock (mesa_wire/bind port no_links)
      ;made (mesa_wire/engine world.self life no_links sock.endpoint keys authority kept_at)
    ? (eq 0 made.floor) (mesa_wire/up "Mesa did not activate the key of this world" idle no_links keys authority 0 0 life 0 no_does)
    = begun (mesa_foot/start world.self rift made.engine sock.endpoint)
      ;fed (mesa_wire/feed routes no_links keys authority held world begun)
      met (mesa_foot/meet (mesa_wire/peers fed.links) fed.locks begun)
      ;opening
        | mesa_foot/after (mesa_foot/acted made.acts.weld(fed.acts) world met)
          \ ready=mesa_foot/foot
          ^ mesa_foot/turn
          | mesa_foot/tick began world ready
    | mesa_wire/up started opening.foot fed.links keys authority sock.fd made.floor life got.at(0) fed.notes.weld(opening.does)
  + run
    ' This function is the foot. It waits for the world as it is now, and
    ' reads its mark. It increases its rift when the world is behind what it
    ' announced before. Then it starts (mesa_wire/raise) and runs the rules
    ' for all that arrives.
    '
    ' The foot listens at the address that it gets at its start
    ' (io/mesa/address), and then it writes that address on its record. If it
    ' gets no address, it listens where its record tells it to listen. When a
    ' port appears on a record that had no port, the foot makes a socket on
    ' that port at that time. A foot keeps its port until it stops.
    '
    ' When Mesa holds a later key for the world than the record states, the
    ' foot writes the life and the key that Mesa holds on the record.
    \ init=feet/task/init self=nat
    ^ nat
    = record (mesa_wire/own init.ax)
      ;handed (mesa_wire/took "io/mesa/address")
    = [host given] (mesa_wire/address handed)
    = port (? (eq 0 given) record.nat_at(['port] 0) given)
      ring (mesa_wire/named record.txt_at(['ring]) "io/mesa/ring")
      file (mesa_wire/named record.txt_at(['mark]) "io/mesa/mark")
      ;folder (Folder "io/mesa")
    ; (mesa_wire/look init.parent)
    = ;world
        % ignored=nat 0
        ^ namespace
        = ;[message caps] (Recv[mesa_wire/task] 0)
        ; (helm/supervisor/close_caps caps)
        ?> (./binary request status body mime) message (_ 0)
        | mesa_wire/world body
      ;found (mesa_wire/marked file)
    ?> (./some was) found (mesa_wire/halt "mesa foot: the mark file is not whole, and the foot does not run" file)
    = rift (? (lt world.local.top was.top) was.rift.inc was.rift)
      seen (nmax was.top world.local.top)
      ;noted (mesa_wire/remark file rift seen was.floor)
    ? !(noted) (mesa_wire/halt "mesa foot: the mark was not written, and the foot does not run" file)
    = began (div (Now 0) 1000000)
      held (mesa_wire/beside ring "authority")
      kept_at (mesa_wire/beside ring "manifest")
      ;up (mesa_wire/raise ring port rift was.floor record.nat_at(['life] 1) (gt record.nat_at(['key] 0) 0) (mesa_wire/routes init.ax) began world)
    ? (gt up.why.len 0) (mesa_wire/halt (str/cat_all ["mesa foot: " up.why]) ring)
    = ;kept (mesa_wire/remark file rift seen up.floor)
      keys up.keys
      authority up.authority
    ? !(kept) (mesa_wire/halt "mesa foot: the mark was not written, and the foot does not run" file)
    ; (mesa_wire/watch up.fd 1)
    ; (? (and (eq record.nat_at(['key] 0) up.public) (eq record.nat_at(['life] 1) up.life)) 0 (Send 0 (mesa_wire/note [world.self 'io 'mesa] ((myth/sing ['key] (pails/n up.public)).put(['life] (pails/n up.life))))))
    ; (? ((eq 0 given) || (and (eq given record.nat_at(['port] 0)) (equal[str] host record.txt_at(['host])))) 0 (Send 0 (mesa_wire/note [world.self 'io 'mesa] (myth/sing ['port] (pails/n given)).put(['host] (pails/t host)))))
    ; (Trace ["mesa foot listening" port] 0)
    ; (mesa_wire/act up.foot.endpoint up.links up.does)
    % foot=mesa_foot/foot up.foot
      view=namespace world
      links=row[mesa_wire/link] up.links
      top=nat seen
      floor=nat up.floor
      last=nat began
      own_life=nat up.life
      at=nat port
      serial=nat 1
      stopping=nat 0
      queue=row[mesa_wire/wish] []
    = ;[message caps] (Recv[mesa_wire/task] 0)
      ticked
        ? message
         > time=mesa_wire/tick .true
        .false
    ; (? ticked 0 (helm/supervisor/close_caps caps))
    ? message
     > wanted=mesa_wire/wish
      ; (? (queue.len) 0 (mesa_wire/ask init.parent [wanted]))
      | _ foot view links top floor last own_life at serial stopping queue.snoc(wanted)
     > (./binary request status body mime)
      = held (mesa_wire/world body)
        no=maybe[iota] .none
        ;refusal (? (eq 1 request) (mesa_wire/plan init.parent queue held) no)
        refused (maybe/is_some refusal)
        rest (? refused queue.drop(1) queue)
        ;answered (? refused (mesa_foot/landed queue.at(0).request refusal held foot) (mesa_foot/quiet foot))
      ; (? refused (mesa_wire/ask init.parent rest) 0)
      ; (mesa_wire/act foot.endpoint links answered.does)
      ? (eq held.local.top view.local.top) (_ answered.foot held links top floor last own_life at serial stopping rest)
      = ;turn (mesa_foot/turned held answered.foot)
        higher (nmax top held.local.top)
        ;kept (? (gt higher top) (mesa_wire/remark file foot.rift higher floor) 1)
      ; (? kept 0 (Trace ["mesa foot: the mark was not written" file] 0))
      ; (mesa_wire/act foot.endpoint links turn.does)
      | _ turn.foot held links (? kept higher top) floor last own_life at serial stopping rest
     > (./fact binding)
      ; (mesa_wire/look init.parent)
      ? (mesa_wire/stopped binding) (_ foot view links top floor last own_life at serial 1 queue)
      = card (mesa_wire/own binding)
        wish card.nat_at(['port] 0)
        opens (and (eq 0 at) (gt wish 0))
        ;sock (? opens (mesa_wire/bind wish links) (mesa_wire/socket foot.endpoint 0))
        ;shut (? opens (MesaClose foot.endpoint) 0)
        port_now (? opens wish at)
        serial_now (? opens serial.inc serial)
      ; (? opens (mesa_wire/watch sock.fd serial_now) 0)
      ; (? opens (Trace ["mesa foot listening" wish] 0) 0)
      = placed foot.set_endpoint(sock.endpoint)
        no_acts=row[row[nat]] []
        no_locks=row[mesa_foot/lock] []
        next_life card.nat_at(['life] 1)
        ;turned (? (gt next_life own_life) (mesa_wire/rotate view.self ring own_life next_life placed.endpoint placed.engine keys authority) (mesa_wire/turned 0 0 no_acts 0))
        life_now (? turned.ok next_life own_life)
        floor_now (? turned.ok turned.floor floor)
      ; (? turned.ok (mesa_wire/remark file placed.rift top floor_now) 0)
      ; (? turned.ok (Send 0 (mesa_wire/note [view.self 'io 'mesa] (myth/sing ['key] (pails/n turned.public)))) 0)
      ; (? (and (gt next_life own_life) !(turned.ok)) (Trace ["mesa foot: the key of this world was not changed" next_life] 0) 0)
      = base (? turned.ok placed.set_locks(no_locks) placed)
        ;fed (mesa_wire/feed (mesa_wire/routes binding) links keys authority held view base)
        met (mesa_foot/meet (mesa_wire/peers fed.links) fed.locks base)
        ;turn (mesa_foot/acted turned.acts.weld(fed.acts) view met)
      ; (mesa_wire/act met.endpoint fed.links fed.notes)
      ; (mesa_wire/act met.endpoint fed.links turn.does)
      | _ turn.foot view fed.links top floor_now last life_now port_now serial_now stopping queue
     > time=mesa_wire/tick
      ? !(eq time.serial serial)
        ; (Send caps.at(0) 0)
        ; (helm/supervisor/close_caps caps)
        | _ foot view links top floor last own_life at serial stopping queue
      ? (gt stopping 0)
        ; (Send caps.at(0) 0)
        ; (helm/supervisor/close_caps caps)
        ; (MesaClose foot.endpoint)
        ; (MesaClose foot.engine)
        0
      = ;came (MesaHear foot.endpoint)
        packets=row[row[nat]] came
        start=mesa_foot/turn (mesa_foot/quiet foot)
        ;heard
          | packets.fold start
            \ ;acc=mesa_foot/turn packet=row[nat]
            ^ mesa_foot/turn
            | mesa_foot/after acc
              \ ready=mesa_foot/foot
              ^ mesa_foot/turn
              | mesa_foot/heard packet.at(0) packet.at(1) packet.at(2) view ready
      ; (mesa_wire/act foot.endpoint links heard.does)
      = due (ge time.now (add last mesa_foot/every))
        ;moved (? due (mesa_foot/tick time.now view heard.foot) (mesa_foot/clock time.now view heard.foot))
      ; (mesa_wire/act foot.endpoint links moved.does)
      = ;next (MesaDeadline foot.engine foot.endpoint)
        ahead (? (le next time.now) 1 (sub next time.now))
        wait
          ? (eq packets.len 64) 1
          ? (eq 0 next) 1000
          ? (gt ahead 1000) 1000
          ahead
      ; (Send caps.at(0) wait)
      ; (helm/supervisor/close_caps caps)
      | _ moved.foot view links top floor (? due time.now last) own_life at serial stopping queue
     > asked=mesa_wire/again
      ? !(eq asked.era foot.era) (_ foot view links top floor last own_life at serial stopping queue)
      = ;made (mesa_wire/engine view.self own_life links foot.endpoint keys authority kept_at)
      ? (eq 0 made.floor)
        ; (MesaClose made.engine)
        ; (Trace ["mesa foot: a new engine was not made" own_life] 0)
        | _ foot view links top floor last own_life at serial stopping queue
      = ;shut (MesaClose foot.engine)
        ;kept (mesa_wire/remark file foot.rift top made.floor)
        raised (mesa_foot/raised made.engine foot)
        ;turn (mesa_foot/acted made.acts view raised)
      ; (mesa_wire/act raised.endpoint links turn.does)
      | _ turn.foot view links top made.floor last own_life at serial stopping queue
     > (./ack err)
      ? !(queue.len) (_ foot view links top floor last own_life at serial stopping queue)
      = ;turn (mesa_foot/landed queue.at(0).request (mesa_wire/why err) view foot)
        rest queue.drop(1)
      ; (mesa_wire/act foot.endpoint links turn.does)
      ; (mesa_wire/ask init.parent rest)
      ; (mesa_wire/look init.parent)
      | _ turn.foot view links top floor last own_life at serial stopping rest
    | _ foot view links top floor last own_life at serial stopping queue
  + here
    ^ path
    []
  + stopped
    ' This function tells if the record of the foot shows that the foot must
    ' stop. This is true when the record is gone or holds `stop`.
    \ binding=axal[saga]
    ^ bool
    ?> (./some version) binding.fil .true
    | gt (unpin version.myth).nat_at(['stop] 0) 0
  + accepted
    ' This function tells if the supervisor accepted a write or a
    ' registration.
    \ reply=feet/task[unit]
    ^ bool
    ? reply
     > (./ack err)
      ? err
       > .none .true
       > (./some _) .false
    .false
  + submit
    ' This function sends one write and tells if the world accepted it.
    \ server=nat target=path change=writ
    ^ bool
    ; (SendCaps server (helm/card/step target change) [0])
    = ;[reply caps] (Recv[feet/task[unit]] 0)
    ; (helm/supervisor/close_caps caps)
    | mesa_wire/accepted reply
  + present
    ' If the world has no record of the foot, this function makes an empty
    ' one. It does not change a record that is there. The port, the key and
    ' all other data that the record holds stay the same. An empty poke makes
    ' no change. The world does not accept an empty poke in one case only:
    ' when there is no record. A make replaces the record.
    \ server=nat target=path
    ^ bool
    ? (mesa_wire/submit server target (writ/poke myth/new)) .true
    | mesa_wire/submit server target (writ/make myth/new)
  + unseated
    ' This function gives the writes that give /<self>/our its hand, for a
    ' world that does not have it. The hand tells the foot of news
    ' (net/herald, net/watch). That name must hold a record: the kernel
    ' counts changes only below a name that holds one.
    '
    ' The function gives no write for what is there. Thus a world that starts
    ' again makes no event.
    \ self=iota state=namespace
    ^ row[[p=path q=writ]]
    = none=row[[p=path q=writ]] []
      ours=path [self 'our]
      held (\ name=path (sovereign_care .x name state).fil)
      handed
        \ name=path
        ^ bool
        ?> (./some record) (held name) .false
        | maybe/is_some record.get(limb_slot)
      one (? (maybe/is_some (held (net/horn self))) none none.snoc([(net/horn self) (writ/make (form/card net/herald (lore/line "Tells the Mesa foot of news")))]))
    ? (handed ours) one
    ? (maybe/is_some (held ours)) one.snoc([ours (writ/poke (net/watch self))])
    | one.snoc([ours (writ/make (net/watch self))])
  + seat
    ' This function gives /<self>/our its hand, if it does not have it
    ' (mesa_wire/unseated).
    \ server=nat self=iota
    ^ bool
    ; (SendCaps server (helm/card/snapshot_bat 0 mesa_wire/whole) [0])
    = ;[reply caps] (Recv[feet/task[unit]] 0)
    ; (helm/supervisor/close_caps caps)
    ?> (./binary request status body mime) reply .false
    | (mesa_wire/unseated self (mesa_wire/world body)).all((\ [name change] (mesa_wire/submit server name change)))
  + start
    ' This function starts the foot of a world: it makes sure that the record
    ' /<self>/io/mesa is there and that /<self>/our has its hand, and it
    ' registers the foot at that path. A world calls this function one time, when it starts. The
    ' mesa mount is the switch that makes a world call it.
    \ server=nat self=iota arguments=row[str]
    ^ nat
    = home=path [self 'io 'mesa]
    ? !(mesa_wire/present server home)
      (error (pails/t "Mesa foot record refused"))
    ? !(mesa_wire/seat server self)
      (error (pails/t "Mesa herald refused"))
    = registration | helm/supervisor/register home mesa_wire/run
    ; (SendCaps server registration [0])
    = ;[reply caps] | Recv[feet/task[unit]] 0
    ; | helm/supervisor/close_caps caps
    ? !(mesa_wire/accepted reply)
      (error (pails/t "Mesa foot registration failed"))
    0
